u.s surgeon general and vice admiral  jerome adams joins us now he's a member  of the white house coronavirus task  force  dr adams it's really good to see you  let's start where david left off with  the schools  there's backlash in florida and as a  you're a doctor of course you're also a  father  what needs to happen for schools to  reopen safely and i realize  different states have different needs  but generally speaking  what needs to be done for the schools to  be okay for kids to go back this august  of this september well good morning gail  and thank you for that question as you  mentioned i have a 16 a 14 and a 10 year  old so this is very personal  to me and what i want people to know is  the biggest  determinant of whether or not we can go  back to school actually has  little to nothing to do with the actual  schools it's your background  transmission rate  and it's why we've told people  constantly that if we want to get back  to school  to worship to uh to regular life  folks need to wear face coverings folks  need to practice social distancing  those public health measures are  actually what's going to lower the  transmission rate and we've seen in  norway  we've seen in denmark when they start  with a low background transmission rate  they were able to safely reopen schools  with minimal  to no transmission among young people  particularly people under the age of 10  or 12. now  cdc has recommendations what do you say  what do you say though doctor  yeah go ahead go ahead no i said cdc has  recommendations for things that you can  do  in schools but again the most important  thing is what we do  outside of schools before we reopen to  lower the transmission rate  the government has a role but i want  people to understand we as individuals  have a role too  to do what we can and that includes  wearing face coverings  okay but you've still got teachers  you've still got parents that say  wearing face coverings  do you think that's really it if your  kids go back to school and they have  face coverings  you think that's that's safe enough oh  no gail that that's  part of the equation when you're in  school what i'm saying is we need to be  doing that now  before school is open so that we can  lower the transmission rate in schools  we also have to protect the older people  the teachers  the uh the family members that these  students may live with because we know  that the risk is low  to the actual students but we know that  they can transmit to  others and so i don't want to in any way  shape or form either diminish  the potential impact to teachers and we  need to take measures to make sure we  can protect those  who are vulnerable either because  they're older or they have chronic  medical conditions  let's talk about testing because we  still seem to have issues with the  testing and now it appears the white  house doesn't want to support extra  funding for testing what's the thinking  behind that  well what i can tell you from our  deliberations on the task force  are that in the last bill there was 10  billion with the b  dollars allocated for testing and only  about 36 million  with an m has been drawn down from that  fund so some of the  the debate is not about whether or not  we need more testing or need more or  need more of that in in communities but  it's really about whether or not  we've even spent the money that's  already been allocated i think we all  agree particularly the physicians on the  task force  that we need to have more testing more  uh more  contact tracing more isolation uh but uh  but again we also have to have a lower  community  prevalence rate and i hate to keep  harping but the american people i need  you to hear me  testing isolation and contact tracing  doesn't work when you've got  out of control spread in communities and  so we need to understand that the way we  get all of this to function  is by getting people to commit to  wearing face coverings to not  getting in crowded gatherings to  maintaining six feet of social distance  that lowers the prevalence and  everything else starts to function  do you think the president is finally on  board about wearing a mask he seems to  be changing his messaging on that  well i'm a physician not a political  pundit and it doesn't do me any good to  get back and back and forth about the  president what i can tell you is i'm  very pleased  that he actually is now uh wearing a  face covering  uh medically he's tested more than  anyone  so from a medical standpoint i  understand why he chooses  not to but but i'm glad that he has said  that this is a patriotic thing to do  that supports your freedom it's  something i've been saying for four  months now  all right and the task force briefings  are going to resume today  will you dr fauci and dr burks be in  attendance  well they're still figuring that out i  know that as they resume  we will be there in our different roles  as folks know i'm a strong advocate for  making sure we are promoting the  the awareness of the impact on  communities of color particularly  african americans and particularly  hispanics  all right so maybe we'll see you later  this afternoon at the briefing thank you  very much dr adams  for your time this morning thank you  gail
 

